<!-- AdGate — one rewarded-ad button, three implementations, chosen at RUNTIME.

     Rewarded video is the earn lane every app in this category runs, and it is the one
     feature whose implementation genuinely differs per platform: iOS and Android call a
     mediation SDK compiled into the build; the web has no such SDK to compile in. Shipping
     one and pretending the other exists is how a template teaches a lie, so the lane is
     PICKED, in JSE, from the two facts DSX publishes about where it is running:

       has('scheme')  — is that module in THIS build?   (the capability plane)
       os             — which renderer is running?      (ios · android · web · macos · …)

     The order matters: capability first, platform second. `has()` answers "can I", `os`
     answers "which words" — a build that ships AdMob on web (the module has a `js` site)
     should take the AdMob lane there too, and asking `os == 'web'` first would have sent it
     down the fallback for no reason.

     THE THREE LANES

       admob    · `has('admob')`    — Core/AdMob, the mediation lane
       metaads  · `has('metaads')`  — Core/MetaAudienceNetwork, the same shape
       house    · everywhere else   — a REAL rewarded video served by this app: a full-screen
                                      creative, a watch requirement the viewer cannot skip
                                      past, and an impression the server records. It sells
                                      the app's own VIP offer, which is exactly what a
                                      publisher shows at zero fill. Nothing here pretends to
                                      be a third-party network, and the card names the lane.

     The house lane is a real `<video>`, not a fake timer. DSX has no timer primitive and
     needs none here: `time=` is a STATE KEY the player writes its own clock into, and
     `on:ended` fires when the clip runs out — so the reward is gated on footage that
     actually played, and a countdown that could be faked by a variable never exists.

     UPSTREAM, NAMED RATHER THAN FAKED (PLAN.md §6.24): the AdMob package publishes a BANNER
     face (`dsx.module.admob.banner`, `<admob.Banner/>`, `<admob.Native/>` — all three
     declared in Conformance/inline-surfaces/admob.json) but its REWARDED face is still a
     legacy registry alias (`displayrewardedad`, listed in
     architecture/proposals/legacy-package.md as pending declaration). There is no declared
     action to call, so this file does not invent one: when the module is present it says so
     and runs the house creative, and `dsx.action.playNetwork` is the single place the real
     call lands the day the face is declared. A guessed module call would compile, do
     nothing, and grant a coin anyway — which is worse than a named gap.

     The server is the only thing that grants. Every lane ends at the same POST, and the
     daily cap lives there; the client only reports which creative it managed to show.

     ── WHAT VIP DOES TO THIS COMPONENT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
     "No ads" is a benefit /vip SELLS, so it has to be a benefit this file DELIVERS — a
     paywall whose promise is not implemented anywhere is the one thing a monetisation
     reference must never demonstrate (the same rule that deleted the priced 1080p ladder,
     PLAN.md §36). `vip="true"` therefore switches the whole placement off: `canPlay` is
     false, so `play` refuses in the ACTION and the sheet can never open, and the card
     renders a member state that CONFIRMS the purchased benefit at the one place in the app
     where it applies. It is deliberately not blank: a benefit the viewer paid for should be
     visible where it takes effect, not merely absent.

     Note what this app's "no ads" honestly is. There are no pre-rolls, no interstitials and
     no banners anywhere — the single ad surface in the product is the OPT-IN rewarded clip
     below. So VIP does not remove an interruption the viewer was suffering; it removes the
     reason to sit through one, because a VIP unlocks every episode at 0 coins and has
     nothing left to earn coins for. The copy says exactly that rather than implying the app
     was ever ad-infested.

     ── APP STORE RULES THIS SURFACE IS BOUND BY ─────────────────────────────────────────
     A rewarded placement is one of the most heavily specified surfaces in App Review, and
     three rules land ON THIS FILE rather than on a policy document somewhere:

       5.1.2(i) — a reward may NOT be conditioned on the viewer enabling a system
                  permission (ATT, notifications, location). Nothing here asks for one, and
                  nothing may: the lane pick reads `has('scheme')`, which is a BUILD fact,
                  never a user grant. The rule is restated on `playNetwork` because that is
                  the seam where a real mediation SDK — and its ATT prompt — will land.
       2.5.18   — an ad must be labelled as an ad, must not trick the viewer into tapping
                  it, and must carry an easily accessible, visible close/skip control large
                  enough to dismiss it. Hence: the "AD" tag, and a 44pt close button that is
                  present from the FIRST FRAME rather than appearing once the reward is
                  earned. The early-close branch in `close` was written from the start and
                  was unreachable until now, because the only control that could fire it was
                  hidden behind `complete`. A countdown the viewer cannot escape is not a
                  skip control.
       2.5.18   — "Apps that contain ads must also include the ability for users to report
                  any inappropriate or age-inappropriate ads." Hence `report`, and the
                  `reported` event, which is the single seam an adopter POSTs from. What the
                  control does in THIS build is named in the UI rather than implied: the
                  creative is suppressed for the rest of the session on this device, and
                  there is no moderation inbox to send it to yet, because no report route is
                  declared server-side. Article 7 — the degradation is named where the
                  viewer reads it. -->
<!-- spacing 0 on the root: a spaced component root pays one spacing at its top on iOS (PLAN.md
     §6.183) — the default 8 sat above the row card as an 8pt phantom -->
<vstack alignItems="stretch" spacing="0">
  <head>
    <attribute as="left" default="0"/>          <!-- ad views remaining today (a server fact) -->
    <attribute as="today" default="0"/>
    <attribute as="cap" default="10"/>
    <attribute as="reward" default="15"/>
    <!-- THE SEAM IS THE DEFAULT. A caller may still pass its own headers, but it no
         longer has to: `global.auth.headers` is what <AuthSeam> publishes, so this part
         reads the one session the app has instead of a copy plumbed down per mount. -->
    <attribute as="headers" default="global.auth.headers"/>
    <!-- The house ad plays from /promo, not /media. /media/* is entitlement-gated now (a
         paid episode's bytes are refused without a ticket), and the house creative is
         nobody's episode — pointing it at an episode file meant the ad itself 403'd and
         played black. /promo aliases the same bytes with no new binary. -->
    <attribute as="creative" default="'/promo/house-ad.mp4'"/>
    <!-- the viewer's entitlement, passed in by the screen that owns the wallet read. A
         markup attribute arrives as a STRING and `default=` is a JSE expression, so the
         consumer accepts BOTH spellings (AGENTS.md) — a component cannot make its callers
         remember which one it wanted, and the cost of getting this wrong is showing ads to
         someone who paid not to see them. -->
    <attribute as="vip" default="false"/>
    <!-- THE OPEN NONCE: a host that wants to start the ad from somewhere other than this
         row (the success modal's "Watch an ad to earn N coins" CTA, spec 9.5) bumps a
         number; the watch below plays on change. A component has no imperative face, so a
         changing value is the one seam a parent can pull — and `play` still refuses through
         `canPlay`, so a bump can never show a member an ad or beat the cap. -->
    <attribute as="open" default="0"/>

    <event as="granted" payload="reward left"/>
    <event as="failed" payload="message"/>
    <!-- 2.5.18's report requirement, as a SEAM rather than a guess: this component knows
         which creative offended and nothing else. The host screen decides what to do with
         it, and an adopter with a moderation route POSTs from there — the same shape as
         `playNetwork` for the mediation call. -->
    <event as="reported" payload="lane creative"/>

    <api as="adCall" url="/rewards/ad" method="POST" auto="false" headers="dsx.attribute.headers"/>

    <variable as="playing">return false</variable>
    <variable as="watched">return 0</variable>     <!-- the clip's own clock, written by `time=` -->
    <variable as="ended">return false</variable>
    <!-- A REWARDED AD IS NOT PAUSABLE. That is the product rule, and on web it is also what
         makes the clip run at all: a `<sheet>` re-parents its content into an overlay portal,
         and moving a `<video>` in the DOM pauses it. Measured: play → playing → pause within
         the same frame at t=1.43, every time the sheet opened. With `paused=` bound two-way
         the renderer writes that pause back into this variable and the clip is latched off
         forever; without the binding nothing ever retries and it stalls at t=0.16.
         So the binding stays, and the watch below re-asserts the rule. `resumes` bounds it:
         if the browser is REFUSING autoplay rather than the portal interrupting it, three
         attempts is enough to know, and the viewer gets a tap-to-play instead of a loop. -->
    <variable as="paused">return true</variable>
    <variable as="resumes">return 0</variable>

    <!-- THE LANE PICK. Capability first, platform second (see the header). -->
    <variable as="lane" computed="true">
      if (has('admob')) { return 'admob' }
      if (has('metaads')) { return 'metaads' }
      return 'house'
    </variable>
    <!-- ...and, separately, whether that lane can actually show a network creative today.
         Split from the pick so the card can say "AdMob is in this build, its rewarded face
         is not declared yet" instead of silently looking like the house lane. -->
    <variable as="networkReady" computed="true">return false</variable>

    <variable as="laneLabel" computed="true">
      if (dsx.variable.lane == 'admob') { return 'AdMob rewarded' }
      if (dsx.variable.lane == 'metaads') { return 'Meta Audience Network' }
      return 'Sponsored · house ad'
    </variable>

    <!-- BOTH SPELLINGS, on purpose (see the attribute). `vip="{{ x }}"` interpolates a real
         boolean; `vip="true"` is four characters of string. Accepting one and silently
         ignoring the other is how a paying member ends up looking at an ad. -->
    <variable as="isVip" computed="true">
      const v = dsx.attribute.vip
      return v === true || v === 'true'
    </variable>
    <!-- the viewer used the 2.5.18 report control. App-wide rather than per-mount so it
         survives leaving and re-entering the Rewards tab: an api cache and a per-mount
         variable both die with the mount (PLAN.md §6.30), and an opt-out that forgets the
         moment you navigate away is not an opt-out. It is a SESSION fact, not a stored
         preference — §6.33 (no cross-platform KV) means there is nowhere durable to put it —
         and the card says "this session" rather than implying it is remembered. -->
    <variable as="optedOut" computed="true">return global.adOptOut == true</variable>

    <!-- DISABLED MEANS DISABLED: `play` refuses through this, so the VIP branch and the
         report branch are guards in the ACTION, not just a grey button. -->
    <variable as="canPlay" computed="true">
      if (dsx.variable.isVip) { return false }
      if (dsx.variable.optedOut) { return false }
      return Number(dsx.attribute.left) > 0 && dsx.variable.playing == false
    </variable>
    <!-- 15s is the category's rewarded length; a shorter clip completes on `on:ended` -->
    <variable as="secsLeft" computed="true">
      const n = 15 - Math.floor(Number(dsx.variable.watched))
      return n < 0 ? 0 : n
    </variable>
    <!-- complete when the clip has played the category's 15s rewarded length, or when it
         ended first — a creative shorter than the requirement still counts as watched -->
    <variable as="complete" computed="true">
      return dsx.variable.ended || Number(dsx.variable.watched) >= 15
    </variable>
    <!-- the browser refused autoplay and we have stopped asking: the viewer's own tap is the
         only thing left that can start it, so say so instead of showing a frozen countdown -->
    <variable as="stalled" computed="true">
      return dsx.variable.playing && dsx.variable.paused && Number(dsx.variable.resumes) >= 3
    </variable>
    <variable as="ctaLabel" computed="true">
      if (dsx.variable.complete) { return 'Claim +' + dsx.attribute.reward }
      return 'Reward in ' + dsx.variable.secsLeft + 's'
    </variable>
    <!-- 2.5.18: the close control is present from the first frame, so its label has to say
         what closing COSTS at the moment it is read — a skip button that silently forfeits
         a reward is exactly the trick the rule exists to prevent. -->
    <variable as="closeLabel" computed="true">
      if (dsx.variable.complete) { return 'Close ad and claim your reward' }
      return 'Close ad — the reward needs the full clip'
    </variable>

    <!-- THE CARD'S HEADLINE, which is a different sentence for each of the three states this
         placement can be in. A member must never read "Watch ads, earn coins". -->
    <variable as="adFace" computed="true">
      if (dsx.variable.isVip) { return false }
      return dsx.variable.optedOut == false
    </variable>
    <variable as="optFace" computed="true">
      if (dsx.variable.isVip) { return false }
      return dsx.variable.optedOut
    </variable>
    <variable as="spent" computed="true">return Number(dsx.attribute.left) <= 0</variable>
    <variable as="rowA11y" computed="true">
      if (dsx.variable.isVip) { return 'Ads are off — included with VIP' }
      if (dsx.variable.optedOut) { return 'Ads are off — you reported this one' }
      return 'Earn rewards: watch a video to earn ' + dsx.attribute.reward + ' coins, ' + dsx.attribute.today + ' of ' + dsx.attribute.cap + ' today'
    </variable>
    <variable as="cardTitle" computed="true">
      if (dsx.variable.isVip) { return 'Ads are off' }
      if (dsx.variable.optedOut) { return 'Ads are off' }
      return 'Earn rewards'
    </variable>

    <!-- ── the lanes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->

    <action as="play">
      if (dsx.variable.canPlay == false) { return }
      if (dsx.variable.lane != 'house' && dsx.variable.networkReady) { dsx.action.playNetwork() }
      else { dsx.action.playHouse() }
    </action>

    <!-- THE MEDIATION LANE. One place, deliberately empty, so the day Core/AdMob declares a
         rewarded action this is a one-line change and nothing else in the app moves. It is
         unreachable today because `networkReady` is false — a fact the card states out loud
         rather than a branch that quietly never runs.

         WHOEVER FILLS THIS IN: App Store 5.1.2(i) — "Your app may not require users to
         enable system functionalities (e.g. push notifications, location services, tracking)
         in order to access functionality, content, use the app, or receive monetary or other
         compensation". A mediation SDK will want ATT. Asking is fine; CONDITIONING THE COIN
         ON THE ANSWER IS NOT. A viewer who declines tracking must still be able to watch a
         (non-personalised) rewarded ad and still be granted the reward — or, if the network
         genuinely returns no fill without consent, the lane must fall through to `playHouse`
         rather than refuse the reward. Nothing in this build asks for a permission today,
         and the daily cap plus the server grant are the only gates on the coin. -->
    <action as="playNetwork">
      dsx.event('failed', { message: dsx.variable.laneLabel + ' has no declared rewarded action yet' })
    </action>

    <!-- THE HOUSE LANE. A real placement: the creative covers the screen and the reward
         button does not exist until the clip has actually played. -->
    <action as="playHouse">
      dsx.variable.watched = 0
      dsx.variable.ended = false
      dsx.variable.resumes = 0
      dsx.variable.paused = false
      dsx.variable.playing = true
    </action>

    <action as="finished">
      dsx.variable.ended = true
    </action>

    <!-- "an ad cannot be paused", enforced. Bounded, so a browser that is refusing autoplay
         outright is not fought in a loop — after three tries the viewer gets a tap target,
         which is a real user gesture and always satisfies the policy. -->
    <action as="resume">
      if (dsx.variable.playing == false) { return }
      if (dsx.variable.complete) { return }
      if (dsx.variable.paused == false) { return }
      if (Number(dsx.variable.resumes) >= 3) { return }
      dsx.variable.resumes = Number(dsx.variable.resumes) + 1
      dsx.variable.paused = false
    </action>
    <action as="tapPlay">
      dsx.variable.resumes = 0
      dsx.variable.paused = false
    </action>
    <!-- a creative that will not load must never hold the viewer hostage: the impression
         failed, so it is reported as a failure and the sheet closes -->
    <action as="broke">
      dsx.variable.paused = true
      dsx.variable.playing = false
      dsx.event('failed', { message: 'The ad failed to load — no reward, and it did not count against your cap' })
    </action>

    <action as="close">
      const done = dsx.variable.complete
      dsx.variable.paused = true
      dsx.variable.playing = false
      if (done) { dsx.action.grant() }
      else { dsx.event('failed', { message: 'Ad closed early — no reward' }) }
    </action>

    <!-- 2.5.18's report control. It does the two things it CAN do honestly — dismiss the ad
         with no penalty (the impression never reaches the server, so the daily cap is
         untouched) and suppress the creative for the rest of the session — and it emits the
         seam an adopter POSTs from. It grants nothing, so it cannot be farmed; and it is
         deliberately not wired to `broke`, because "this ad is inappropriate" and "this ad
         failed to load" are different facts and a moderation queue needs to tell them
         apart. -->
    <!-- THE HOUSE AD'S ONE LINK OUT, and it closes the cover first. `href="/vip"` pushed
         the route and left this cover sheet painted over the page it had just mounted —
         the router does not consult the overlay ledger, so no navigation closes an open
         modal (measured on two other surfaces this pass; PLAN.md 6.95). Here it was worse
         than a stuck sheet: the ad video would have gone on playing behind it.
         It routes through `close()` rather than a bare `playing = false` so the ad is
         accounted for exactly as the X accounts for it — a viewer who leaves for the
         paywall part-way through has not earned the reward, and `close()` is the one place
         that decides. And a ROUTE is right here: /vip is a destination, and someone who
         taps out of an ad to read a paywall is done with the ad. -->
    <action as="seePlans">
      dsx.action.close()
      // RESET, not push: /vip mounts the tab bar, and a push slides the whole frame with
      // the bar riding along — the founder's rule, and PLAN.md 6.29's reasoning from the
      // product side. A screen with a menu bar is entered laterally, never pushed.
      dsx.module.route.reset({ path: '/vip' })
    </action>
    <action as="report">
      global.adOptOut = true
      dsx.variable.paused = true
      dsx.variable.playing = false
      dsx.event('reported', { lane: dsx.variable.lane, creative: dsx.attribute.creative })
    </action>

    <!-- Every lane ends here. The client reports that an impression completed; the SERVER
         owns the cap, the ledger and the grant, and a rejection comes back as a declared
         reason (`r.data.message`), never as a transport error. -->
    <action as="grant">
      const r = await adCall.send({ lane: dsx.variable.lane })
      if (r.ok) { dsx.event('granted', { reward: r.data.reward, left: r.data.left }) }
      else { dsx.event('failed', { message: r.data != null && r.data.message != null ? r.data.message : 'No ad available' }) }
    </action>

    <watch value="dsx.variable.paused" on:change="dsx.action.resume()"/>
    <watch value="dsx.attribute.open" on:change="dsx.action.play()"/>

    <!-- THE RESTING FACE IS A ROW CARD (docs/design/reference-ui-spec.md 9.3 row 3): the
         44 tile, "Earn rewards (n/N)", "Watch a video to earn N coins", the Watch pill — the
         same recipe every other Earn Rewards row wears (Components/parts/RewardRow.dsx), so
         the ad lane sits in the list as one row of it. -->
    <style as="row" background="#161616" radius="12" paddingH="16" paddingV="15" spacing="8" alignItems="center" minHeight="74"/>
    <style as="tile" width="44" height="44" radius="22" background="#2A2A2A" alignItems="center" alignY="center"/>
    <style as="col" spacing="3" alignItems="stretch"/>
    <style as="titleRow" spacing="6" alignItems="center"/>
    <style as="title" fontSize="17" color="#FFFFFF" lineLimit="1"/>
    <style as="count" fontSize="15" color="#8E8E93" lineLimit="1"/>
    <style as="sub" fontSize="15" color="#8E8E93" lineLimit="2" lineSpacing="2"/>
    <style as="pill" minWidth="75" height="31" radius="8" paddingH="10" alignItems="center" alignY="center"/>
    <style as="pillTxt" fontSize="15" fontWeight="semibold" lineLimit="1"/>
    <style as="lanePill" background="rgba(255,255,255,0.08)" radius="10" paddingH="9" paddingV="4" fontSize="11" fontWeight="semibold" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.72)"/>
    <style as="vipPill" background="rgba(246,182,61,0.16)" radius="10" paddingH="9" paddingV="4" spacing="5" alignItems="center"/>
    <style as="vipPillTxt" fontSize="11" fontWeight="bold" color="#F6B63D"/>

    <style as="adTag" background="rgba(0,0,0,0.6)" radius="4" paddingH="7" paddingV="2" fontSize="11" fontWeight="bold" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" tracking="1"/>
    <style as="countPill" background="rgba(0,0,0,0.6)" radius="12" paddingH="10" paddingV="5" fontSize="12" fontWeight="semibold" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.9)"/>
    <!-- 44pt, both of them. 2.5.18 asks for a close control "large enough for people to
         easily dismiss the ad" and the house design bar puts the floor at 44 — the close
         button shipped at 32×32, which is the size the rule is written about. The report
         control is sized to the same floor rather than to its text. -->
    <style as="closeBtn" width="44" height="44" radius="22" background="rgba(0,0,0,0.6)" alignItems="center"/>
    <style as="reportBtn" height="44" radius="22" paddingH="14" spacing="6" background="rgba(0,0,0,0.6)" alignItems="center"/>
    <style as="reportTxt" fontSize="12" fontWeight="semibold" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)"/>
    <style as="pitch" spacing="10" alignItems="center" paddingH="26" paddingBottom="22"/>
    <style as="kicker" fontSize="11" fontWeight="bold" color="#FFE9A8" tracking="3"/>
    <style as="pitchTitle" fontSize="24" fontWeight="bold" color="#FFFFFF" textAlign="center"/>
    <style as="pitchBody" fontSize="13" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.75)" textAlign="center" lineSpacing="3"/>
    <style as="claimBtn" background="#FF2C55" radius="24" paddingH="32" paddingV="13" fontSize="15" fontWeight="bold" color="#FFFFFF"/>
    <style as="waitBtn" background="rgba(255,255,255,0.14)" radius="24" paddingH="32" paddingV="13" fontSize="15" fontWeight="semibold" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.55)"/>
  </head>

  <!-- SELF-SUFFICIENT: this part calls the shared design vocabulary (Components/parts/
       Theme.dsx), and a caller must not have to remember to register it first. The
       block is app-wide and last-write-wins, so a second mount costs a 0x0 stack. -->
  <Theme/>
  <!-- the row: the whole card is the target (the reference's 31pt pill is under the 44
       floor), and `play` refuses in the action for a member, an opt-out or a spent cap -->
  <hstack class="row" on:tap="dsx.action.play()" a11yGroup="true" a11yTrait="button" a11yLabel="{{ dsx.variable.rowA11y }}">
    <vstack class="tile" style="justify-content: center; flex: none">
      <image icon="{{ dsx.variable.isVip ? 'crown.fill' : 'play.rectangle.fill' }}" iconSize="19" color="{{ warmGold() }}" a11yHidden="true"/>
    </vstack>
    <vstack class="col" grow="width" style="flex: 1; min-width: 0">
      <hstack class="titleRow">
        <text value="{{ dsx.variable.cardTitle }}" class="title" style="white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis"/>
        <!-- the daily counter is a fact about an ad lane the member does not have; showing
             "0/15" to a VIP would re-introduce the thing the pass removed -->
        <text value="({{ dsx.attribute.today }}/{{ dsx.attribute.cap }})" class="count" style="white-space: nowrap"
              visible-if="dsx.variable.adFace"/>
      </hstack>
      <text value="{dsx.attribute.reward, plural, one {Watch a video to earn # coin} other {Watch a video to earn # coins}}" class="sub"
            visible-if="dsx.variable.adFace" style="display: -webkit-box; -webkit-line-clamp: 2; -webkit-box-orient: vertical; overflow: hidden"/>
      <!-- THE MEMBER FACE. Not blank: a benefit someone paid for should be visible at the
           place it takes effect, and this is that place. -->
      <text value="Your VIP pass switches the rewarded clip off. Check-in, the wheel and the tasks still pay." class="sub" visible-if="dsx.variable.isVip"
            style="display: -webkit-box; -webkit-line-clamp: 2; -webkit-box-orient: vertical; overflow: hidden"/>
      <!-- Article 7, named where the viewer reads it -->
      <text value="This creative will not play again this session; the report goes to the moderation queue." class="sub" visible-if="dsx.variable.optFace"
            style="display: -webkit-box; -webkit-line-clamp: 2; -webkit-box-orient: vertical; overflow: hidden"/>
    </vstack>
    <hstack class="vipPill" visible-if="dsx.variable.isVip" a11yHidden="true" style="flex: none">
      <image icon="crown.fill" iconSize="11" color="{{ gold() }}" a11yHidden="true"/>
      <text value="VIP" class="vipPillTxt"/>
    </hstack>
    <vstack class="pill" style="flex: none; justify-content: center" visible-if="dsx.variable.isVip == false"
            background="{{ dsx.variable.canPlay ? brand() : 'rgba(255,44,85,0.2)' }}">
      <text value="Off" class="pillTxt" visible-if="dsx.variable.optedOut" color="{{ accent() }}" style="white-space: nowrap"/>
      <text value="Done" class="pillTxt" visible-if="dsx.variable.optedOut == false &amp;&amp; dsx.variable.spent" color="{{ accent() }}" style="white-space: nowrap"/>
      <text value="Watch" class="pillTxt" visible-if="dsx.variable.optedOut == false &amp;&amp; dsx.variable.spent == false" color="{{ ink() }}" style="white-space: nowrap"/>
    </vstack>
  </hstack>

  <!-- the creative. `mode="cover"` is a full-screen modal on every renderer, which is what a
       rewarded placement is on all three.

       BRIDGED, LOUDLY (upstream PLAN.md §6.22): `inset` is a declared `<sheet>` attribute
       (stack-elements.json: number, default 14) and the WEB renderer ignores it — measured,
       `.dsx-sheet-content` keeps `padding: 12px 16px 16px` and no sheet-inset custom
       property is emitted at all, so the creative rendered in a 343x812 box inside a 375pt screen with black
       rails down both sides and its own overflow covering the pitch copy. A rewarded
       placement is edge to edge on every network there is, so the layer cancels the pad
       here — and `inset="0"` STAYS on the tag, so this becomes a no-op the day the renderer
       honours it rather than a hack nobody can find again. -->
  <sheet present="dsx.variable.playing" mode="cover" background="#000000" inset="0">
    <zstack style="margin: -12px -16px -16px; width: calc(100% + 32px)">
      <video src="{{ dsx.attribute.creative }}" active="{{ dsx.variable.playing }}"
             muted="true" loop="false" gravity="fill" audio="ambient"
             paused="dsx.variable.paused" time="dsx.variable.watched"
             on:ended="dsx.action.finished()" on:error="dsx.action.broke()"
             a11yLabel="Sponsored message for ShortDrama VIP"
             height="{{ dsx.screen.height }}" style="width: 100%"/>

      <!-- explicit zIndex: a `<video>` establishes its own stacking context, so a later plain
           sibling does NOT reliably paint over it (the same law as a transformed sibling —
           AGENTS.md). Without this the clip covered the pitch copy. -->
      <vstack style="z-index: 2; width: 100%; height: {{ dsx.screen.height }}px; justify-content: space-between; pointer-events: none; background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(0,0,0,0.55) 0%, rgba(0,0,0,0) 22%, rgba(0,0,0,0.35) 58%, rgba(0,0,0,0.92) 100%)">
        <!-- THE AD CHROME, built to 2.5.18: the label, the report control, the counter and a
             close target that is present from the first frame. It used to appear only once
             `complete` was true, which made the countdown inescapable and left the
             early-close branch in `close` permanently unreachable. -->
        <hstack spacing="8" style="padding: 16px; align-items: center; pointer-events: auto">
          <text value="AD" class="adTag"/>
          <!-- WHICH LANE is playing, said on the creative itself (the lane paragraph that
               used to sit under the button has no room in a 74pt row — Article 7 keeps the
               name where the viewer is looking) -->
          <text value="{{ dsx.variable.laneLabel }}" class="lanePill" visible-if="dsx.variable.lane == 'house'"/>
          <hstack class="reportBtn" on:tap="dsx.action.report()"
                  a11yGroup="true" a11yTrait="button"
                  a11yLabel="Report this ad as inappropriate. It closes with no reward.">
            <image icon="exclamationmark.triangle.fill" iconSize="12" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" a11yHidden="true"/>
            <text value="Report" class="reportTxt"/>
          </hstack>
          <spacer/>
          <text value="{{ dsx.variable.secsLeft }}s" class="countPill" visible-if="dsx.variable.complete == false"
                a11yLabel="{{ dsx.variable.secsLeft }} seconds before the reward"/>
          <vstack class="closeBtn" style="justify-content: center"
                  on:tap="dsx.action.close()" a11yTrait="button" a11yLabel="{{ dsx.variable.closeLabel }}">
            <image icon="xmark" iconSize="16" color="#FFFFFF" a11yHidden="true"/>
          </vstack>
        </hstack>

        <vstack class="pitch" style="pointer-events: auto">
          <text value="SHORTDRAMA VIP" class="kicker"/>
          <text value="Every episode. No coins." class="pitchTitle"/>
          <!-- THE HOUSE AD IS STILL AN AD, AND IT IS STILL THIS APP'S WORD. It used to close
               with "…and drops bonus coins into your wallet every week", which no action in
               `server/*.dsx` does — VIP writes `vip_until` and nothing else (server/store.dsx
               :150-153). A false claim is not more forgivable for being inside a creative;
               it is less, because a viewer buys against it. Both clauses below are checkable:
               `unlockEpisode` prices at 0 for a VIP (server/wallet.dsx:190-192), and this
               component switches itself off above. -->
          <text value="VIP unlocks every episode at zero coins and switches this ad off. The coins you already have stay in your wallet." class="pitchBody" style="max-width: 330px"/>
          <text value="{{ dsx.variable.ctaLabel }}" class="claimBtn" visible-if="dsx.variable.complete"
                a11yTrait="button" on:tap="dsx.action.close()"/>
          <text value="{{ dsx.variable.ctaLabel }}" class="waitBtn"
                visible-if="dsx.variable.complete == false && dsx.variable.stalled == false"/>
          <text value="Tap to play the ad" class="claimBtn"
                visible-if="dsx.variable.stalled" a11yTrait="button" on:tap="dsx.action.tapPlay()"/>
          <text value="See VIP plans" class="lanePill" a11yTrait="button"
                a11yLabel="See VIP plans — closes this ad"
                on:tap="dsx.action.seePlans()"/>
        </vstack>
      </vstack>
    </zstack>
  </sheet>
</vstack>
